    <!-- Plain-language explanations, one per measurement -->
    <string name="explain_heart_rate">The middle value of every beat-to-beat rate in this recording. A resting adult usually sits between 60 and 100 bpm, and fit people are often lower.</string>
    <string name="explain_heart_rate_range">The slowest and the fastest single beat in the recording. A gap between them is normal — the heart speeds up slightly when you breathe in.</string>
    <string name="explain_rr_mean">The average gap between beats, and how much that gap moved. Doctors call this the RR interval.</string>
    <string name="explain_sdnn">How much the gap between beats varied across the whole recording. It reflects the balance between the nerves that speed the heart up and slow it down. Over 30 seconds it is a rough indication, not a health score.</string>
    <string name="explain_rmssd">How much each beat differed from the one just before it. It responds mainly to the nerve that slows the heart, and it moves with rest, stress and breathing.</string>
    <string name="explain_pnn50">The share of consecutive beats that differed by more than 50 milliseconds. Another way of describing beat-to-beat variation.</string>
    <string name="explain_beat_count">How many heartbeats were found in the parts of the recording clean enough to read.</string>
    <string name="explain_corrected_intervals">Gaps between beats that were left out of the calculations because they were too far from the others to be a real heartbeat — usually a movement artefact. The second number is how many gaps were examined.</string>
    <string name="explain_detector_agreement">Two independent beat detectors ran over this recording. This is how often they picked the same beat. Low agreement means the numbers above are less certain.</string>
    <string name="explain_analysed_duration">How much of the recording was clean enough to measure, out of its full length.</string>

